SuperCPN
========

SuperCPN is a pob220 fork of OpenCPN focused on a cleaner helm UI and a tighter
weather, current, and routing workflow. It keeps OpenCPN plugin compatibility
where practical, while separating the visible app identity, commands, desktop
metadata, data directories, and configuration paths from OpenCPN.


What Changed From OpenCPN
-------------------------

SuperCPN branding
  The app name, launcher, command names, installed data paths, local config
  paths, and desktop metadata are branded as SuperCPN so SuperCPN and OpenCPN
  can live side by side on the same machine.

Cleaner left sidebar
  The old narrow icon-only toolbar is now a fixed-size left sidebar with icons
  and text labels. It stays docked to the left, does not grow taller as tools
  are added, and scrolls when more tools are present than fit vertically.

Boat Profile selector
  The active Boat Profile is visible beside the main sidebar. Selecting it opens
  a compact menu of saved profiles and profile actions.

Boat Profiles
  SuperCPN has first-class Boat Profiles for vessel dimensions, cruising speed,
  max speed, planning duration, current step, grid spacing, current provider,
  and model/data directory defaults.

Weather and current routing
  The bundled GRIB/current planning flow reads the active Boat Profile directly.
  Current-GRIB defaults and generated metadata are derived from the selected
  vessel profile instead of generic defaults.

Onboarding
  First-run setup includes Boat Profile creation so SuperCPN has useful vessel
  data before weather/current planning begins.

OpenCPN import placement
  OpenCPN import is exposed from onboarding and Display > General settings,
  rather than from the quick profile selector.


Build On A Regular Linux PC
---------------------------

These commands are intended for Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint, Raspberry Pi OS, or
similar apt-based systems.

Install build tools and dependencies:

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -y \
      build-essential cmake git gettext pkg-config \
      libarchive-dev libbz2-dev libcurl4-openssl-dev libdrm-dev libelf-dev \
      libexif-dev libgdk-pixbuf-2.0-dev libglew-dev libgtk-3-dev \
      liblz4-dev liblzma-dev libpango1.0-dev libshp-dev libsqlite3-dev \
      libssl-dev libtinyxml-dev libudev-dev libusb-1.0-0-dev \
      libwxgtk3.2-dev libwxgtk-webview3.2-dev libwxsvg-dev \
      portaudio19-dev python-is-python3 python3-dbus python3-gi rapidjson-dev

If your distro does not provide wxWidgets 3.2 packages, use the equivalent
libwxgtk3.0-gtk3-dev and libwxgtk-webview3.0-gtk3-dev packages.

Configure and build:

    git clone https://github.com/pob220/SuperCPN.git
    cd SuperCPN
    cmake -S . -B build \
      -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
      -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    cmake --build build -j"$(nproc)"

Run from the build tree:

    ./build/supercpn

Install locally:

    cmake --build build --target install -j"$(nproc)"

After install, launch with:

    supercpn


Raspberry Pi Build
------------------

For a Raspberry Pi 4 or Pi 5 running Raspberry Pi OS 64-bit, the regular Linux
instructions usually work. The Pi-optimized approach below uses fewer parallel
jobs and disables OpenGL if the graphics stack is unstable.

Install dependencies:

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -y \
      build-essential cmake git gettext pkg-config \
      libarchive-dev libbz2-dev libcurl4-openssl-dev libdrm-dev libelf-dev \
      libexif-dev libgdk-pixbuf-2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev \
      liblz4-dev liblzma-dev libpango1.0-dev libshp-dev libsqlite3-dev \
      libssl-dev libtinyxml-dev libudev-dev libusb-1.0-0-dev \
      libwxgtk3.2-dev libwxgtk-webview3.2-dev libwxsvg-dev \
      portaudio19-dev python-is-python3 python3-dbus python3-gi rapidjson-dev

Configure a lighter Pi build:

    git clone https://github.com/pob220/SuperCPN.git
    cd SuperCPN
    cmake -S . -B build-pi \
      -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local" \
      -DOCPN_USE_GL=OFF
    cmake --build build-pi -j2

Install on the Pi:

    cmake --build build-pi --target install -j2

If your Pi has good OpenGL support and you want hardware acceleration, remove
-DOCPN_USE_GL=OFF and rebuild:

    cmake -S . -B build-pi-gl \
      -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local"
    cmake --build build-pi-gl -j2


Tests
-----

Run the focused Boat Profile tests:

    ./build/test/tests --gtest_filter='BoatProfile.*'

Run all available tests:

    cmake --build build --target run-tests


Notes
-----

SuperCPN intentionally keeps compatibility-sensitive OpenCPN plugin APIs and
identifiers where changing them would break normal plugin behavior.

Some bundled plugin translation files still use upstream OpenCPN plugin message
catalog names for compatibility.

OpenCPN profile import currently has UI entry points in onboarding and
Display > General settings. The import backend should be expanded as OpenCPN
profile sources are formalized.
